## Files
- src/tools/geocode-location.ts (create)

## What to do
Port the geocoding tool from taina-v3-2 as a standalone module. This tool converts text location descriptions to GPS coordinates using OpenStreetMap Nominatim API.

**Export a function (NOT a Vercel AI SDK tool — just a plain async function):**

```typescript
export interface GeocodeResult {
  success: true;
  latitude: number;
  longitude: number;
  formattedAddress: string;
  locality?: string;
  country?: string;
  countryCode?: string;
  stateProvince?: string;
}

export interface GeocodeError {
  success: false;
  error: string;
}

export type GeocodeResponse = GeocodeResult | GeocodeError;

export async function geocodeLocation(query: string, countryCode?: string): Promise<GeocodeResponse>
```

**Behavior:**
- Calls `https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org/search` with params: `q`, `format=json`, `limit=1`, `addressdetails=1`
- If countryCode provided, adds `countrycodes` param (lowercased)
- Sets `User-Agent: Pi-Taina/1.0 (biodiversity-bot)`
- Parses first result: extracts lat/lon as numbers, display_name, address components
- Returns GeocodeResult on success, GeocodeError on failure (no results, network error, non-200 status)
- Handles: empty results array, fetch errors, non-200 responses

## Dont
- Do NOT use the Vercel AI SDK `tool()` wrapper — this is a plain function
- Do NOT import from taina-v3-2 — rewrite from scratch using the same Nominatim API
- Do NOT add rate limiting (Nominatim has its own)

## Files
- src/tools/identify-species.ts (create)

## What to do
Create a species identification function that analyzes a photo using Google Gemini (vision-capable). This is ported from taina-v3-2 but adapted for Gemini instead of Claude.

**Export:**

```typescript
export interface ImageQuality {
  overall: "excellent" | "good" | "fair" | "poor";
  sharpness: "sharp" | "slightly_blurry" | "blurry";
  distance: "close_up" | "medium" | "far" | "too_far";
  lighting: "good" | "acceptable" | "poor";
  keyFeaturesVisible: string[];
  keyFeaturesMissing: string[];
  suggestions: string[];
  suitableForPublication: boolean;
}

export interface SpeciesIdentification {
  commonName: string;
  scientificName: string;
  taxonomy: {
    kingdom: string;
    phylum: string;
    class: string;
    order: string;
    family: string;
  };
  confidence: "high" | "medium" | "low";
  conservationStatus: string;
  nativeRegions: string[];
  habitat: string;
  description: string;
  ecologicalRole: string;
  possibleAlternatives: string[];
  organismGroup: "plant" | "fungus" | "bird" | "mammal" | "insect" | "reptile" | "amphibian" | "marine" | "other";
  recommendedPhotos: string[];
  imageQuality: ImageQuality;
}

export interface IdentificationError {
  error: string;
  suggestion?: string;
}

export type IdentificationResult = SpeciesIdentification | IdentificationError;

// imageData: base64-encoded image data
// mimeType: e.g. "image/jpeg"
// userContext: optional notes from user (location, habitat, behavior)
// apiKey: Gemini API key
// model: Gemini model ID (default from EnvConfig.speciesIdModel)
export async function identifySpecies(
  imageData: string,
  mimeType: string,
  apiKey: string,
  model?: string,
  userContext?: string
): Promise<IdentificationResult>
```

**Behavior:**
- Uses `@google/generative-ai` SDK (Google Generative AI for Node.js)
- Creates `GoogleGenerativeAI` client with the provided apiKey
- Uses model from parameter, defaulting to `"gemini-2.5-flash"`
- Sends the image as inline_data (base64 + mimeType) along with the identification prompt
- Set `responseMimeType: "application/json"` in generationConfig to get structured JSON output
- The identification prompt should request JSON output with all fields from SpeciesIdentification
- Include the full organism-group-specific image quality evaluation instructions from taina-v3-2 (plants, birds, insects, fungi, reptiles, marine)
- Parse the JSON response, return typed result
- On JSON parse failure: return { commonName: "Unknown", scientificName: "Unknown", confidence: "low", description: rawText, ... } with defaults
- On API error: return { error: "Failed to analyze image", suggestion: "Try a clearer photo" }

## Dont
- Do NOT use Anthropic SDK — use `@google/generative-ai`
- Do NOT use Vercel AI SDK
- Do NOT import from taina-v3-2
- Do NOT handle Telegram-specific logic (downloading photos etc) — that is handled by the caller
- Do NOT read process.env — accept apiKey and model as parameters

## Files
- src/tools/publish-occurrence.ts (create)

## What to do
Create a function that publishes a Darwin Core occurrence record to the community ATProto PDS. Ported from taina-v3-2 but simplified for the community account model.

**Export:**

```typescript
export interface TelegramUser {
  id: number;           // Telegram user ID (stable, numeric)
  username?: string;    // @username (can change)
  displayName: string;  // First name + last name
}

export interface OccurrenceInput {
  scientificName: string;
  vernacularName?: string;
  basisOfRecord?: string; // default "HumanObservation"

  // Location (at least one of GPS or text required)
  decimalLatitude?: number;
  decimalLongitude?: number;
  coordinateUncertaintyInMeters?: number;
  locality?: string;
  country?: string;
  countryCode?: string;

  // Optional
  eventDate?: string;  // ISO 8601, defaults to today
  habitat?: string;
  behavior?: string;
  individualCount?: number;
  occurrenceRemarks?: string;

  // Image (raw bytes, will be uploaded to PDS as blob)
  imageData?: Buffer;
  imageMimeType?: string;

  // Who submitted this (Telegram user info)
  submittedBy: TelegramUser;
}

export interface PublishResult {
  success: true;
  uri: string;
  cid: string;
  scientificName: string;
  vernacularName?: string;
  location: string;
  date: string;
  hasImage: boolean;
}

export interface PublishError {
  success: false;
  error: string;
}

export type PublishResponse = PublishResult | PublishError;

export async function publishOccurrence(input: OccurrenceInput): Promise<PublishResponse>
```

**Behavior:**
- Calls `getAtprotoAgent()` from `../atproto.ts` to get the authenticated agent
- Validates: must have either GPS coords or text location, must have scientificName
- Builds record with `$type: "app.gainforest.dwc.occurrence"`
- Sets `recordedBy` to: `"{displayName} (@{username}, tg:{id})"` — all three Telegram user identifiers. If username is undefined, omit the @username part.
- Sets `recordedByID` to `"telegram:{id}"` 
- Sets `rightsHolder` to the community handle (from getCommunityHandle())
- If imageData provided: uploads to PDS via `agent.uploadBlob(imageData, { encoding: imageMimeType })`, attaches as `imageEvidence: [{ file: blobRef }]`
- Serializes blob ref through JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(blob)) to avoid CID serialization issues (same pattern as taina-v3-2)
- Publishes via `agent.com.atproto.repo.createRecord({ repo: did, collection: "app.gainforest.dwc.occurrence", record })`
- Sets `eventDate` to today if not provided
- Sets `createdAt` to ISO timestamp
- Sets `geodeticDatum: "EPSG:4326"` when GPS coords present
- Sets `occurrenceStatus: "present"`

## Dont
- Do NOT use OAuth — uses the community account from atproto.ts
- Do NOT accept image URLs — accept raw Buffer (the Telegram bot layer downloads the photo and passes bytes)
- Do NOT import from taina-v3-2
- Do NOT add multiple image support yet — single image is fine for MVP

## Files
- src/telegram.ts (create)

## What to do
Create the Telegram bot layer using grammY. This module handles all Telegram-specific logic: receiving messages, downloading photos, extracting locations, and sending responses.

**Export:**

```typescript
export interface IncomingMessage {
  chatId: number;
  messageId: number;
  text?: string;
  // Photo data (if user sent a photo)
  photo?: {
    data: Buffer;
    mimeType: string;  // always "image/jpeg" from Telegram
    fileId: string;
  };
  // Location (if user sent a Telegram location)
  location?: {
    latitude: number;
    longitude: number;
  };
  // Telegram user info
  user: {
    id: number;
    username?: string;
    displayName: string;  // first_name + (last_name || "")
  };
  // Whether this is a group chat
  isGroup: boolean;
  // Whether the bot was mentioned (in groups)
  isMentioned: boolean;
}

// Callback for processing messages
export type MessageHandler = (msg: IncomingMessage) => Promise<void>;

// Create and start the bot
// Returns a function to send replies
export async function createTelegramBot(onMessage: MessageHandler): Promise<{
  reply: (chatId: number, text: string, options?: { replyToMessageId?: number; parseMode?: "HTML" | "Markdown" | "MarkdownV2" }) => Promise<void>;
  sendPhoto: (chatId: number, photo: Buffer, caption?: string) => Promise<void>;
  stop: () => void;
}>
```

**Behavior:**

1. **Bot creation**: Reads `TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN` from env. Throws if missing.

2. **Message filtering**:
   - **DMs** (private chats): ALL messages forwarded to handler
   - **Groups**: ONLY forward if:
     - Bot is @mentioned in the text, OR
     - Message contains a photo (auto-detect observation), OR
     - Message contains a location
   - Strip the @botname mention from text before forwarding

3. **Photo handling**:
   - When user sends a photo, Telegram provides multiple sizes. Pick the largest (last in `msg.photo` array).
   - Download the file using `bot.api.getFile(fileId)` then fetch from `https://api.telegram.org/file/bot{token}/{filePath}`
   - Pass raw Buffer + "image/jpeg" as mimeType (Telegram always converts to JPEG)

4. **Location handling**:
   - Telegram `location` messages have `{ latitude, longitude }` — extract directly
   - Also handle `venue` messages (they include location)

5. **User info extraction**:
   - `displayName`: `from.first_name + " " + (from.last_name || "")` trimmed
   - `username`: `from.username` (may be undefined)
   - `id`: `from.id`

6. **Reply function**:
   - Wraps `bot.api.sendMessage()` with parse_mode support
   - Default parse_mode: "HTML" (supports <b>, <i>, <code>)
   - Handles Telegram message length limit (4096 chars) by splitting into multiple messages if needed

7. **Start**: Uses long polling (`bot.start()`)

8. **Error handling**: 
   - `bot.catch()` logs errors but does not crash
   - Individual message handler errors are caught and a "Sorry, something went wrong" reply is sent

## Dont
- Do NOT add any AI/agent logic — this is purely the Telegram transport layer
- Do NOT import Pi SDK here
- Do NOT add inline keyboards yet — that comes in a later task
- Do NOT use webhooks — use long polling for self-hosted simplicity

## Files
- src/index.ts (modify — replace placeholder)

## What to do
Wire everything together in the main entry point. This is the startup script that initializes all components and connects the Telegram bot to the Pi agent.

**Behavior:**

```typescript
import "dotenv/config";
import { createTelegramBot } from "./telegram.js";
import { sendToAgent, disposeAllSessions } from "./agent.js";
import { getAtprotoAgent } from "./atproto.js";

async function main() {
  // 1. Validate required env vars exist (fail fast)
  const required = ["TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN", "ANTHROPIC_API_KEY"];
  for (const key of required) {
    if (!process.env[key]) {
      console.error(`Missing required env var: ${key}`);
      process.exit(1);
    }
  }

  // 2. Initialize ATProto (optional — warn if not configured, dont crash)
  const atprotoConfigured = process.env.ATPROTO_HANDLE && process.env.ATPROTO_PASSWORD;
  if (atprotoConfigured) {
    try {
      await getAtprotoAgent();
      console.log("✅ ATProto community account connected");
    } catch (err) {
      console.warn("⚠️  ATProto login failed:", err.message);
      console.warn("   Observation publishing will be unavailable");
    }
  } else {
    console.warn("⚠️  ATProto not configured (ATPROTO_HANDLE / ATPROTO_PASSWORD missing)");
    console.warn("   Observation publishing will be unavailable");
  }

  // 3. Start Telegram bot
  console.log("🤖 Starting Tainá Telegram bot...");
  const bot = await createTelegramBot(async (msg) => {
    try {
      // Send typing indicator
      // (grammY: bot.api.sendChatAction(msg.chatId, "typing"))
      // Note: the bot object is in closure, but we need to send typing
      // from inside the handler. The reply function is available.

      // Process message through Pi agent
      const response = await sendToAgent(msg);

      // Send response back to Telegram
      if (response && response.trim()) {
        await bot.reply(msg.chatId, response, {
          replyToMessageId: msg.isGroup ? msg.messageId : undefined,
        });
      }
    } catch (err) {
      console.error("Error processing message:", err);
      await bot.reply(msg.chatId, "Sorry, I encountered an error. Please try again. 🙏");
    }
  });

  console.log("✅ Tainá is running! Press Ctrl+C to stop.");

  // 4. Graceful shutdown
  const shutdown = async () => {
    console.log("\n🛑 Shutting down...");
    bot.stop();
    await disposeAllSessions();
    process.exit(0);
  };

  process.on("SIGINT", shutdown);
  process.on("SIGTERM", shutdown);
}

main().catch((err) => {
  console.error("Fatal error:", err);
  process.exit(1);
});
```

**Key points:**
- Fail fast on missing TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN and ANTHROPIC_API_KEY
- ATProto is optional — warn but dont crash if not configured
- Graceful shutdown on SIGINT/SIGTERM
- In groups, reply to the original message (replyToMessageId)
- In DMs, dont quote-reply (cleaner UX)
- Log startup status clearly with emoji indicators

## Dont
- Do NOT add any business logic — this is pure wiring
- Do NOT add inline keyboards or complex Telegram features
- Do NOT add health checks or HTTP servers
- Do NOT add database connections
